92186576411 true] [17592186529265 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139538347 17592186576411 false] [17592186529265 1071 "Consider the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-4x + 4y &=-9 \\\\ 2x - y &=-4\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThis system of equations has {{field-1}}." 13194139538347 17592186576411 true] [17592186529269 1078 #edb/ref 17592186529312 13194139538347 17592186576411 true] [17592186529270 1078 #edb/ref 17592186529291 13194139538347 17592186576411 true] [17592186529271 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139538347 17592186576411 true] [17592186529271 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139538347 17592186576411 false] [17592186529271 1071 "Consider the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2x + y &=2 \\\\ 4x + 2y &=5\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThis system of equations has {{field-1}}." 13194139538347 17592186576411 true] [17592186529271 1074 "Let's start by solving the system using elimination.\n\nNotice that if we multiply the first equation by $-2$, then we will get a $-4x$ term that can cancel with the $4x$ term in the second equation:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2x + y &=2 && \\mid \\cdot (-2) \\\\ 4x + 2y &=5\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWhen we add the two equations, it turns out that both variables cancel, and we obtain a false statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-4x - 2y &= -4 \\\\\n4x + 2y &= 5 \\\\\n0 &= (-4 + 5) \\\\\n0 &= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe statement $0=1$ is *always false*. Therefore, we conclude that the system has *no solutions*." 13194139538347 17592186576411 true] [17592186529281 1078 #edb/ref 17592186529268 13194139538347 17592186576411 true] [17592186529282 1078 #edb/ref 17592186529315 13194139538347 17592186576411 true] [17592186529287 1078 #edb/ref 17592186529305 13194139538347 17592186576411 true] [17592186529290 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139538347 17592186576411 true] [17592186529290 1071 "Consider the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2x - 3y &=10 \\\\ 2x - y &=6\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThis system of equations has {{field-1}}." 13194139538347 17592186576411 false] [17592186529290 1071 "Consider the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2x - 3y &=10 \\\\ 4x - 2y &=12\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThis system of equations has {{field-1}}." 13194139538347 17592186576411 true] [17592186529290 1074 "Let's start by solving the system using elimination.\n\nNotice that if we multiply the first equation by $-1$, then we will get a $-2x$ term that can cancel with the $2x$ term in the second equation:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2x - 3y &=10 && \\mid \\cdot (-1) \\\\ 2x - y &=6\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWhen we add the two equations, the $x$ terms cancel, and we can solve for $y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-2x + 3y &= -10 \\\\\n2x - y &= 6 \\\\\n2y &= (-10 + 6) \\\\\n2y &= -4 \\\\\ny &= -2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting into the first equation gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2x + (-3)(-2) &= 10 \\\\\n2x &= 4 \\\\\nx &= 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe system has *a single solution*, $(2,-2)$." 13194139538347 17592186576411 false] [17592186529290 1074 "Let's start by solving the system using elimination.\n\nNotice that if we multiply the first equation by $-2$, then we will get a $-4x$ term that can cancel with the $4x$ term in the second equation:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2x - 3y &=10 && \\text{multiply by } -2 \\\\ 4x - 2y &=12\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWhen we add the two equations, the $x$ terms cancel, and we can solve for $y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-4x + 6y &= -20 \\\\\n4x - 2y &= 12 \\\\\n4y &= (-20 + 12) \\\\\n4y &= -8 \\\\\ny &= -2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting into the first equation gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2x + (-3)(-2) &= 10 \\\\\n2x &= 4 \\\\\nx &= 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe system has *a single solution*, $(2,-2)$.\n\nThe corresponding lines intersect at exactly one point." 13194139538347 17592186576411 true] [17592186529294 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139538347 17592186576411 false] [17592186529294 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139538347 17592186576411 true] [17592186529294 1071 "Consider the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}3x + y &=0 \\\\ -3x + 5y &=-2\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThis system of equations has {{field-1}}." 13194139538347 17592186576411 false] [17592186529294 1071 "Consider the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}6x + 2y &=0 \\\\ -3x + 5y &=-2\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThis system of equations has {{field-1}}." 13194139538347 17592186576411 true] [17592186529294 1074 "Let's start by solving the system using elimination.\n\nNotice that if we multiply the first equation by $1$, then we will get a $3x$ term that can cancel with the $-3x$ term in the second equation:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}3x + y &=0 && \\mid \\cdot (1) \\\\ -3x + 5y &=-2\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWhen we add the two equations, the $x$ terms cancel, and we can solve for $y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n3x + y &= 0 \\\\\n-3x + 5y &= -2 \\\\\n6y &= (0 - 2) \\\\\n6y &= -2 \\\\\ny &= - \\frac{1}{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting into the first equation gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n3x + (1)\\left(- \\frac{1}{3}\\right) &= 0 \\\\\n3x &= \\frac{1}{3} \\\\\nx &= \\frac{1}{9}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe system has *a single solution*, $(\\frac{1}{9},- \\frac{1}{3})$." 13194139538347 17592186576411 false] [17592186529294 1074 "Let's start by solving the system using elimination.\n\nNotice that if we multiply the second equation by $2$, then we will get a $-6x$ term that can cancel with the $6x$ term in the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}6x + 2y &=0 \\\\ -3x + 5y &=-2 && \\text{multiply by } 2\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWhen we add the two equations, the $x$ terms cancel, and we can solve for $y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n6x + 2y &= 0 \\\\\n-6x + 10y &= -4 \\\\\n12y &= (0 - 4) \\\\\n12y &= -4 \\\\\ny &= - \\frac{1}{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting into the first equation gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n6x + (2)\\left(- \\frac{1}{3}\\right) &= 0 \\\\\n6x &= \\frac{2}{3} \\\\\nx &= \\frac{1}{9}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe system has *a single solution*, $(\\frac{1}{9},- \\frac{1}{3})$.\n\nThe corresponding lines intersect at exactly one point." 13194139538347 17592186576411 true] [17592186529306 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139538347 17592186576411 false] [17592186529306 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139538347 17592186576411 true] [17592186529306 1071 "Consider the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-3x - y &=8 \\\\ 3x + y &=-6\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThis system of equations has {{field-1}}." 13194139538347 17592186576411 false] [17592186529306 1071 "Consider the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-3x - y &=8 \\\\ 6x + 2y &=-12\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThis system of equations has {{field-1}}." 13194139538347 17592186576411 true] [17592186529306 1074 "Let's start by solving the system using elimination.\n\nNotice that if we multiply the first equation by $1$, then we will get a $-3x$ term that can cancel with the $3x$ term in the second equation:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-3x - y &=8 && \\mid \\cdot (1) \\\\ 3x + y &=-6\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWhen we add the two equations, it turns out that both variables cancel, and we obtain a false statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-3x - y &= 8 \\\\\n3x + y &= -6 \\\\\n0 &= (8 - 6) \\\\\n0 &= 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe statement $0=2$ is *always false*. Therefore, we conclude that the system has *no solutions*." 13194139538347 17592186576411 false] [17592186529306 1074 "Let's start by solving the system using elimination.\n\nNotice that if we multiply the first equation by $2$, then we will get a $-6x$ term that can cancel with the $6x$ term in the second equation:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-3x - y &=8 && \\text{multiply by } 2 \\\\ 6x + 2y &=-12\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWhen we add the two equations, it turns out that both variables cancel, and we obtain a false statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-6x - 2y &= 16 \\\\\n6x + 2y &= -12 \\\\\n0 &= (16 - 12) \\\\\n0 &= 4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe statement $0=4$ is *always false*. Therefore, we conclude that the system has *no solutions*.\n\nThe corresponding lines are parallel and do not intersect." 13194139538347 17592186576411 true] [17592186529316 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139538347 17592186576411 false] [17592186529316 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139538347 17592186576411 true] [17592186529316 1071 "Consider the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-x - 5y &=-12 \\\\ 2x - 6y &=-4\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThis system of equations has {{field-1}}." 13194139538347 17592186576411 true] [17592186529316 1071 "Consider the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-x - 5y &=-12 \\\\ x - 3y &=-2\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThis system of equations has {{field-1}}." 13194139538347 17592186576411 false] [17592186529316 1074 "Let's start by solving the system using elimination.\n\nNotice that if we multiply the first equation by $1$, then we will get a $-x$ term that can cancel with the $x$ term in the second equation:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-x - 5y &=-12 && \\mid \\cdot (1) \\\\ x - 3y &=-2\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWhen we add the two equations, the $x$ terms cancel, and we can solve for $y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-x - 5y &= -12 \\\\\nx - 3y &= -2 \\\\\n-8y &= (-12 - 2) \\\\\n-8y &= -14 \\\\\ny &= \\frac{7}{4}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting into the first equation gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-x + (-5)\\left(\\frac{7}{4}\\right) &= -12 \\\\\n-x &= - \\frac{13}{4} \\\\\nx &= \\frac{13}{4}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe system has *a single solution*, $(\\frac{13}{4},\\frac{7}{4})$." 13194139538347 17592186576411 false] [17592186529316 1074 "Let's start by solving the system using elimination.\n\nNotice that if we multiply the first equation by $2$, then we will get a $-2x$ term that can cancel with the $2x$ term in the second equation:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-x - 5y &=-12 && \\text{multiply by } 2 \\\\ 2x - 6y &=-4\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWhen we add the two equations, the $x$ terms cancel, and we can solve for $y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-2x - 10y &= -24 \\\\\n2x - 6y &= -4 \\\\\n-16y &= (-24 - 4) \\\\\n-16y &= -28 \\\\\ny &= \\frac{7}{4}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting into the first equation gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-x + (-5)\\left(\\frac{7}{4}\\right) &= -12 \\\\\n-x &= - \\frac{13}{4} \\\\\nx &= \\frac{13}{4}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe system has *a single solution*, $(\\frac{13}{4},\\frac{7}{4})$.\n\nThe corresponding lines intersect at exactly one point." 13194139538347 17592186576411 true] [17592186529320 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139538347 17592186576411 true] [17592186529320 1071 "Consider the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2x - y &=4 \\\\ 2x - y &=4\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThis system of equations has {{field-1}}." 13194139538347 17592186576411 false] [17592186529320 1071 "Consider the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}4x - 2y &=8 \\\\ 2x - y &=4\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThis system of equations has {{field-1}}." 13194139538347 17592186576411 true] [17592186529320 1074 "Let's start by solving the system using elimination.\n\nNotice that if we multiply the first equation by $-1$, then we will get a $-2x$ term that can cancel with the $2x$ term in the second equation:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2x - y &=4 && \\mid \\cdot (-1) \\\\ 2x - y &=4\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWhen we add the two equations, it turns out that both variables cancel, and we obtain a true statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-2x + y &= -4 \\\\\n2x - y &= 4 \\\\\n0 &= (-4 + 4) \\\\\n0 &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe statement $0=0$ is *always true*. Therefore, we conclude that the system has *infinitely many solutions*." 13194139538347 17592186576411 false] [17592186529320 1074 "Let's start by solving the system using elimination.\n\nNotice that if we multiply the second equation by $-2$, then we will get a $-4x$ term that can cancel with the $4x$ term in the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}4x - 2y &=8 \\\\ 2x - y &=4 && \\text{multiply by } -2\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWhen we add the two equations, it turns out that both variables cancel, and we obtain a true statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n4x - 2y &= 8 \\\\\n-4x + 2y &= -8 \\\\\n0 &= (8 - 8) \\\\\n0 &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe statement $0=0$ is *always true*. Therefore, we conclude that the system has *infinitely many solutions*.\n\nThe corresponding lines coincide." 13194139538347 17592186576411 true] [17592186529325 1078 #edb/ref 17592186529302 13194139538347 17592186576411 true] [17592186529326 1078 #edb/ref 17592186529327 13194139538347 17592186576411 true] [17592186529330 1078 #edb/ref 17592186529329 13194139538347 17592186576411 true] [17592186561198 1078 #edb/ref 17592186561225 13194139538347 17592186576411 true] [17592186561213 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139538347 17592186576411 true] [17592186561213 1071 "Consider the lines $l_{1}$ and $l_{2}$, defined by\n\n$$\n\\begin{alignedat}{2}\nl_{1}:\\quad & 5x - 2y &{}={}& -1 \\\\[6pt]\nl_{2}:\\quad & -10x + 4y &{}={}& 2\n\\end{alignedat}\n$$\n\nAt how many points do these lines intersect?\n\nThe lines $l_{1}$ and $l_{2}$ {{field-1}}." 13194139538347 17592186576411 true] [17592186561213 1071 "Consider the lines $l_{1}$ and $l_{2}$, defined by\n\n$$\n\\begin{alignedat}{2}\nl_{1}:\\quad & 5x - 2y &{}={}& -1 \\\\[6pt]\nl_{2}:\\quad & -5x + 2y &{}={}& 1\n\\end{alignedat}\n$$\n\nAt how many points do these lines intersect?\n\nThe lines $l_{1}$ and $l_{2}$ {{field-1}}." 13194139538347 17592186576411 false] [17592186561213 1074 "The number of points of intersection between the two lines is equal to the number of solutions of the corresponding system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}5x - 2y &=-1 \\\\ -10x + 4y &= 2\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe will solve the system using substitution. First, we solve for $y$ in the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n5x - 2y &=-1 \\\\\n-2y &= -5x - 1 \\\\\ny &= \\frac{-5x - 1}{-2} \\\\\ny &= \\frac{5}{2}x + \\frac{1}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{5}{2}x + \\frac{1}{2}\n$$\n\ninto the second equation and solve for $x$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-10x + 4y &= 2 \\\\\n-10x + 4\\left(\\frac{5}{2}x + \\frac{1}{2}\\right) &= 2 \\\\\n-10x + 10x + 2 &= 2 \\\\\n2 &= 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe result is a statement that's always true. Therefore, we conclude that the system has infinitely many solutions.\n\nConsequently, the lines intersect at infinitely many points (i.e., they coincide)." 13194139538347 17592186576411 true] [17592186561213 1074 "The number of points of intersection between the two lines is equal to the number of solutions of the corresponding system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}5x - 2y &=-1 \\\\ -5x + 2y &= 1\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe will solve the system using substitution. First, we solve for $y$ in the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n5x - 2y &=-1 \\\\\n-2y &= -5x - 1 \\\\\ny &= \\frac{-5x - 1}{-2} \\\\\ny &= \\frac{5}{2}x + \\frac{1}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{5}{2}x + \\frac{1}{2}\n$$\n\ninto the second equation and solve for $x$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-5x + 2y &= 1 \\\\\n-5x + 2\\left(\\frac{5}{2}x + \\frac{1}{2}\\right) &= 1 \\\\\n-5x + 5x + 1 &= 1 \\\\\n1 &= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe result is a statement that's always true. Therefore, we conclude that the system has infinitely many solutions.\n\nConsequently, the lines intersect at infinitely many points (i.e., they coincide)." 13194139538347 17592186576411 false] [17592186640186 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139538347 17592186576411 